%N A399979 a(n) is the least missing value from the Heraclitus transform of the first n positive square numbers.
%C A399979 The Heraclitus transform of the first n positive square numbers (as defined in A383442), say h_n = H({1^2, ..., n^2}), cannot have both infinitely many negative terms and infinitely many positive terms as this would imply oscillating infinitely many times around 0 and so arbitrary large steps (which is by design impossible), hence the sequence is well defined (there are always missing values).
%C A399979 We use the well-ordering 0 < 1 < -1 < 2 < -2 < ... to determine the least missing value.
%C A399979 The first k > 0 terms of h_{n+1} that do not require steps of magnitude > n^2 equal the first k terms of h_n.
%C A399979 Empirically, h_n is always infinite, and will contain either all negative or all positive integers and the first differences of h_n are ultimately periodic.
%C A399979 The observed ultimate periods of the first differences of h_n are (+1^2), (-1^2), (+2^2, -1^2, -1^2, +2^2, -1^2) and (-2^2, +1^2, +1^2, -2^2, +1^2); there might be others.
%C A399979 The limit of h_n as n tends to infinity is A377091.
%C A399979 The present sequence is unbounded iff A377091 contains every integer.
